--TEST--
A `Core\Heap<T>` holds one entry per `push` — duplicates and equal keys included — and `peek`/`pop` on an empty one throw rather than answering `null` — docs/spec/01-core-library.md § 9, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R5, `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Heap::push, Core\Heap::peek, Core\Heap::pop, Core\Heap::count, Core\Heap::isEmpty
class Task implements Comparable {
    public string $name;
    public int $cost;

    public function constructor(string $name, int $cost) {
        $this->name = $name;
        $this->cost = $cost;
    }

    public function compareTo(Task $other): int {
        return $this->cost - $other->cost;
    }
}

// § 9 gives the class an `isEmpty`, and `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R5 bans the `peekOrNull`
// twin a `?T` return would invite — so the empty heap is a throw, and the
// message names the member that asked. `pop`'s spelling of it is pinned by
// `heap-orders-by-comparable-or-by-its-comparator.nvst`; this is `peek`'s.
var $h = new Core\Heap<int>();
echo "fresh ", $h->count(), " [", $h->isEmpty(), "]\n";
try {
    $h->peek();
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// A heap is a priority queue, not a set: three pushes of one value are three
// entries, and all three come back out.
$h->push(5);
$h->push(5);
$h->push(5);
echo "duplicates ", $h->count(), " -> ", $h->pop(), $h->pop(), $h->pop(), "\n";

// Draining restores the empty state rather than leaving a heap that merely
// counts zero: the same throw, and then the same heap takes a push again.
echo "drained ", $h->count(), " [", $h->isEmpty(), "]\n";
try {
    $h->pop();
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "empty again\n";
}
$h->push(2);
echo "reused ", $h->count(), " ", $h->peek(), "\n";

// The *same object* pushed twice is held twice, not deduplicated — `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality`
// makes `==` over two objects identity, so this asks whether both pops
// answer the one object that went in.
var $tasks = new Core\Heap<Task>();
var $once = new Task("solo", 2);
$tasks->push($once);
$tasks->push($once);
echo "held ", $tasks->count(), "\n";
var $first = $tasks->pop();
var $second = $tasks->pop();
echo "identity ", $first == $once, $second == $once, $first == $second, "\n";

// Equal keys: `compareTo` answering 0 orders neither before the other, so the
// order among the three is not something a binary heap promises. What § 9
// does promise is that every one of them comes out — asserted as the key each
// carries and the count, never as the names' order.
var $ties = new Core\Heap<Task>();
$ties->push(new Task("a", 3));
$ties->push(new Task("b", 3));
$ties->push(new Task("c", 3));
int $costs = 0;
int $drawn = 0;
while (!$ties->isEmpty()) {
    var $each = $ties->pop();
    $costs = $costs + $each->cost;
    $drawn = $drawn + 1;
}
echo "ties ", $drawn, " x ", $costs, "\n";

// `count` is the entries array's own — there is no second size to fall out of
// step — so it survives interleaving, and `peek` tracks the smallest as
// elements arrive under and over the current root.
var $mix = new Core\Heap<int>();
$mix->push(4);
$mix->push(9);
echo "mix ", $mix->count(), "/", $mix->peek(), " ";
echo "pop=", $mix->pop(), " ";
$mix->push(1);
echo $mix->count(), "/", $mix->peek(), " ";
$mix->push(7);
echo $mix->count(), "/", $mix->peek(), " ";
echo "rest=", $mix->pop(), $mix->pop(), $mix->pop(), " ", $mix->count(), " [", $mix->isEmpty(), "]\n";
--EXPECT--
fresh 0 [1]
Core\Heap::peek() on an empty heap; `isEmpty()` is the question that has an answer for one
duplicates 3 -> 555
drained 0 [1]
empty again
reused 1 2
held 2
identity 111
ties 3 x 9
mix 2/4 pop=4 2/1 3/1 rest=179 0 [1]
